# GRADUATED in R44 from `known_gaps/` — this is now a LIVE regression fixture.
#
# ⚠ SCOPE, stated so the NAME cannot over-claim (Core #12). This fixture is
# ONE CELL of a 7-cell matrix: emitter x channel.
#     BODY channel   -> list . set . dict-KEY . dict-VALUE   (4 hoist calls)
#     FILTER channel -> list . set . dict                    (1 hoist, at the
#                                                             shared producer)
# This is the list x BODY cell. The other six live in
# `cow_loop_bare_param_comprehension_matrix.gg`.
# (An earlier header here claimed a "list/string/dict/set" FOUR-EMITTER class.
# That was wrong twice: there is no string EMITTER -- a `String` source flows
# through the LIST emitter -- and the dict emitter carries TWO body-channel
# expressions, key and value, so a value-only hoist leaves the key side wrong.)
#
# THE SHAPE: `f(Vector[int] xs)` takes a BARE by-value param, so by
# CoW-default-borrow `xs` is a borrow alias into the caller's `a` until it is
# mutated. The loop-carried mutation must be materialized ONCE in the loop's
# PRE-HEADER: not never (the write-through reaches the caller) and not per
# iteration (the clone lands in the re-executing body and is thrown away).
# The comprehension emitters synthesize their own loop and so needed the same
# `materialize_loop_carried_bare_params` hoist the statement-`for` performs.
#
# CORRECT OUTPUT `2 / 2 / 4`: a private copy of [1,2,3,4], two drains -> len 2,
# ys has 2 elements, and the caller still sees 4. The statement-`for` form of
# this exact program prints `2 / 2 / 4` on both lanes and is the oracle.
#
# RED-verified pre-fix on one build of each compiler: Rust `gg` printed
# `3 / 2 / 4`, the self-host driver `0 / 2 / 4`.
#
# Wired test: `cow_loop_bare_param_comprehension` (tests/integration.rs).
int drain_one(Vector[int] &v):
    v.pop()
    return v.len()

void f(Vector[int] xs):
    Vector[int] ys = [drain_one(&xs) for i in 0..2]
    print(xs.len())
    print(ys.len())

void main():
    Vector[int] a = [1, 2, 3, 4]
    f(a)
    print(a.len())
